<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Simulambuco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1885</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Simulambuco was signed on 1 February 1885 between Portugal and the princes and chiefs of Cabinda, on the Atlantic coast north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>. It placed the area, once ruled by the kingdoms of Ngoyo and Kakongo, under Portuguese protection, securing Portugal's claim against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>. Cabinda became an exclave of Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and modern Cabindan separatists still cite the treaty.</p></div>
